---
import GuideLayout from '../../layouts/GuideLayout.astro';
import ShopButtons from '../../components/ShopButtons.astro';
import CoachTip from '../../components/CoachTip.astro';
import { GLOVE_CHART_ROWS, MITT_CHART_ROWS, rangeLabel } from '../../lib/sizing';

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'What size glove does an 8-year-old need?',
    a: 'Most 7–8 year olds use a 10½–11″ glove. Go toward the smaller end if it’s their first season or they have small hands.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What does RHT mean on a baseball glove?',
    a: 'RHT stands for Right-Hand Throw. It’s for players who throw with their right hand, so the glove is worn on the <strong>left</strong> hand. LHT (Left-Hand Throw) gloves are worn on the right hand.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Should I buy a bigger glove so it lasts longer?',
    a: 'No. An oversized glove is heavy and hard to close, so kids drop more balls and lose confidence. Buy the size that fits now. Kids usually need a new glove every one to two seasons anyway.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can a youth player use an adult glove?',
    a: 'Adult gloves have wider finger slots and stiffer leather. For most kids under 12, a “youth pattern” glove fits and closes much better.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does my kid need a position-specific glove?',
    a: 'Not until about age 10 or later. Young players rotate positions, so an all-around glove is best. Catchers (and regular first basemen, 9+) are the exceptions.',
  },
];

const sources = [
  { label: 'Rawlings: determine your glove size by age', href: 'https://www.rawlings.com/product/37GG.html' },
  { label: 'Little League®: what to wear and bring to practice', href: 'https://www.littleleague.org/university/articles/get-the-gear-and-what-to-wear-for-little-league-practices/' },
];
---
<GuideLayout
  slug="youth-glove-size-chart"
  metaTitle="Youth Baseball Glove Size Chart by Age & Position (RHT vs LHT)"
  description="The right glove size for every age and position, from tee ball to high school, plus how to tell RHT from LHT and how a glove should fit."
  faqs={faqs}
  sources={sources}
  related={['how-to-break-in-a-glove', 'youth-bat-size-chart', 'catchers-gear-for-kids']}
>
  <Fragment slot="answer">
    <p>
      <strong>Tee ball (4–6):</strong> 9–10½″. <strong>Ages 7–8:</strong> about 10½–11″. <strong>Ages 9–10:</strong> 11–11½″. <strong>Ages 11–12:</strong>
      11¼–12″ depending on position. A kid who throws right-handed needs an <strong>RHT</strong> glove, which goes on the <strong>left</strong> hand.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <h2 id="chart">Youth glove size chart by age and position</h2>
  <p>
    Gloves are measured from the tip of the index finger, down the inside of the glove, to the heel. Infielders use smaller gloves for quick transfers. Outfielders use
    longer gloves to reach fly balls. Until about age 10, an “all-around” glove is the right pick.
  </p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Age</th>
          <th scope="col">All-around</th>
          <th scope="col">Infield</th>
          <th scope="col">Outfield</th>
          <th scope="col">Pitcher</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          GLOVE_CHART_ROWS.map((r) => (
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">{r.ages}</th>
              <td>
                <strong>{rangeLabel(r.allaround)}</strong>
              </td>
              <td>{rangeLabel(r.infield)}</td>
              <td>{rangeLabel(r.outfield)}</td>
              <td>{rangeLabel(r.pitcher)}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="muted">Based on manufacturer age charts (Rawlings and others). Brands vary by a quarter inch or so.</p>

  <h3 id="mitts">Catcher’s and first base mitts</h3>
  <p>Catcher’s mitts are measured around the outside (circumference), so the numbers are much bigger. First base mitts are long and scoop-shaped.</p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th scope="col">Age</th><th scope="col">Catcher’s mitt</th><th scope="col">First base mitt</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          MITT_CHART_ROWS.map((r) => (
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">{r.ages}</th>
              <td>{rangeLabel(r.catcher)}</td>
              <td>{r.firstbase ? rangeLabel(r.firstbase) : 'Use a regular glove'}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="hand">RHT vs LHT: which hand does the glove go on?</h2>
  <div class="callout">
    <p><strong>The glove goes on the hand they do NOT throw with.</strong></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Throws right-handed → buy “RHT” (Right-Hand Throw).</strong> The glove goes on the left hand. This is about 9 out of 10 kids.</li>
      <li><strong>Throws left-handed → buy “LHT” (Left-Hand Throw).</strong> The glove goes on the right hand.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
  <p>
    Not sure which hand they throw with? Roll them a ball and say “throw it back!” Do it a few times. Young kids sometimes switch, but most settle on one hand quickly. Some
    kids bat one way and throw the other, and that’s fine. For the glove, only the <em>throwing</em> hand matters.
  </p>
  <CoachTip>
    <p>Lefty gloves are harder to find in stores, especially in small youth sizes. Shopping online gives you far more choices.</p>
  </CoachTip>

  <h2 id="fit">How a youth glove should fit</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>One-hand squeeze:</strong> they can open and close it with just their glove hand. If they need two hands, it’s too big or too stiff.</li>
    <li><strong>Fingers in the slots:</strong> no big empty space at the fingertips. (Older players often slide their index finger outside the glove; that’s normal.)</li>
    <li><strong>Shake test:</strong> point the glove at the ground and shake. It should stay on. Snug up the wrist strap if it has one.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="features">What to look for</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>“Youth pattern” or “youth fit”:</strong> narrower finger slots and a smaller wrist opening.</li>
    <li><strong>Soft out of the box:</strong> labels like “game ready” or “pre-broken-in.” Stiff, adult-grade leather can take weeks and frustrates young kids.</li>
    <li><strong>Adjustable wrist strap:</strong> a Velcro strap helps the smallest hands.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>Web style:</strong> for beginners, any web works (a closed or basket web is a bit more forgiving). Pitchers want a closed web to hide their grip; infielders
      like an I-web or H-web; outfielders like an H-web or trapeze.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="mistakes">Common glove mistakes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Buying big to “grow into” it. This is the #1 mistake.</li>
    <li>Buying the wrong hand. RHT goes on the left hand.</li>
    <li>Skipping the break-in. Even soft gloves need a week of catch. See <a href="/guides/how-to-break-in-a-glove/">how to break in a glove</a>.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="shop">Shop youth gloves</h2>
  <p>Searches are pre-filled for youth gloves. Pick the size from the chart above.</p>
  <ShopButtons query="youth baseball glove right hand throw" what="RHT youth gloves" item="guide-glove-rht" />
  <div style="height:.75rem"></div>
  <ShopButtons query="youth baseball glove left hand throw" what="LHT (lefty) gloves" item="guide-glove-lht" />
</GuideLayout>
